::CW_U03_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Scene plan: Central idea: maturity includes accepting guidance. Source influence: a guide gives an object before a journey. Character conflict: Maya thinks leaders work alone, while her teammate thinks leaders trust the group. Which choice best explains integration?{
=A. The plan is integrated because every element supports shared responsibility.#Correct. The central idea, guide-object pattern, and perspective conflict all point to the same meaning.
~B. The plan is integrated because it has many unrelated parts.#Incorrect. Integration requires connection, not just many parts.
~C. The source influence distracts because objects can never develop theme.#Incorrect. Objects can support theme when connected to conflict and growth.
~D. The conflict should be removed so the theme is easier.#Incorrect. Conflict helps develop the theme. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] Passage: Jalen believed the mural had to be his design alone. When Amara handed him a sketch showing how everyone's ideas could fit, he erased his signature from the center and redrew the wall as a shared map. What changed understanding is shown?{
~A. Jalen learns maps are never useful.#Incorrect. The shared map becomes meaningful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Jalen decides art has no theme.#Incorrect. The mural develops theme. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Jalen avoids all collaboration.#Incorrect. He accepts Amara's input. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Jalen learns that leadership can include shared creative ownership.#Correct. He moves from control to collaboration. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Scene goal: develop the idea that memory can guide a person forward. Which source influence best supports the goal?{
~A. A random storm appears with no character effect.#Incorrect. The storm does not support the central idea by itself.
=B. A guide gives the character a family recipe before a move.#Correct. The guide-object pattern connects memory to future action.
~C. A character changes names only.#Incorrect. Name changes do not build the idea.
~D. A scene lists furniture with no memory connection.#Incorrect. The details do not support the idea.
}

::CW_U03_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] A student writes a fiction plan with a theme about honesty, a source influence about a magic doorway, and a character conflict about winning a race. None of the pieces affect each other. What is the best feedback?{
~A. Keep it because unrelated pieces are always stronger.#Incorrect. Unrelated pieces weaken focus. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove the theme from the plan.#Incorrect. Theme can guide integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Revise so the source influence, conflict, and theme all support one central idea.#Correct. Integrated fiction needs connected choices. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Add more unrelated objects.#Incorrect. More unrelated material increases the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Passage: Nia thought asking for help meant failure. Her grandmother gave her an old compass and said, "Even a compass needs a hand to hold it steady." Nia carried it to rehearsal and asked the director where to begin again. What source element and growth arc work together?{
=A. Guide-object pattern and a shift from refusing help to accepting guidance.#Correct. The compass supports the character's changed understanding.
~B. A random object and no character change.#Incorrect. Nia's action changes by the end.
~C. A theme about cooking and a conflict about weather.#Incorrect. Neither appears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A copied source with no transformation.#Incorrect. The compass is adapted into a new realistic scene.
}
